  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Express $3{cos}^{2}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-3{sin}^{2}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{12})$ as a single trigonometric ratio.",
  "worked_solution": "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n3({cos}^{2}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-{sin}^{2}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{12}))\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}\u2061x-{sin}^{2}\u2061x=\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-{sin}^{2}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({cos}^{2}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-{sin}^{2}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{12})) & =3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
